    f.write("\n## 17. In-Sample Testing & The Leaky Bucket Flaw (July 2026)\n")
    f.write("**The Problem:** The ML models (XGBoost, FIGS) continued to generate massive False Positives (>70% of rows). We initially hypothesized this was due to distribution drift between the `normal.csv` and `merged.csv` datasets.\n")
    f.write("**The Evaluation:** To isolate the root cause, we ran an in-sample evaluation of XGBoost and FIGS strictly on the first 150,000 rows of the pristine `normal.csv` dataset (the exact data they were trained on). The results were catastrophic: XGBoost (at 1.5x RMSE) flagged 75.35% of the normal rows as anomalous, and FIGS (without K-Means) flagged 71.64%.\n")
    f.write("**The Discovery (The Leaky Bucket Spams):** The evaluation proved that the models fail to explain their own training distribution. Standard physical noise constantly breached the tight thresholds. More importantly, we identified a critical architectural flaw in the 'Leaky Bucket' delay implementation: once the 15-second warning threshold is reached, the model fires a distinct False Positive for *every single consecutive row* rather than grouping them into a single event. Thus, a single 60-second pump stabilization phase produces 45 separate False Positives, artificially inflating the FP row-count into the tens of thousands.\n")
    f.write("**The Next Step:** We are beginning empirical tuning, starting with reverting XGBoost to a gold-standard 6.0x RMSE threshold, before addressing the leaky bucket's lack of a cooldown/grouping mechanism.\n")
